\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The witness edge is entrywise positive after rescaling}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Use the witness matrix of \ref{fdip-012P}, in the indicator
basis of the #{12} double cosets. Let #{s_j} be the size of the
#{j}-th coset and #{\beta_j} its positive pairing value. In the
rescaled basis given by dividing the #{j}-th indicator by the
positive square root of #{s_j}, the matrix is symmetric. Its
diagonal entry is #{\beta_j^2(1+s_j)} and its off-diagonal entry
is #{\beta_m\beta_j\sqrt{s_m s_j}}. Every entry is positive, so
the support graph is the complete graph on these #{12} indices.}

\proof{
\p{The kernel of \ref{fdip-012P} equals #{2} at the identity and
#{1} elsewhere, so it is unchanged by inversion. \ref{fdip-012T}
gives self-adjointness for counting. On indicators #{e_j}, the
matrix #{M} of \ref{fdip-012P} satisfies
##{\langle e_m,\transferop e_j\rangle=s_m M_{mj},}
because #{\transferop e_j} is constant on the #{m}-th coset.
The cited matrix has full diagonal entry
#{\beta_j^2(1+s_j)}, so the diagonal counting product is
#{s_j\beta_j^2(1+s_j)}. Off the diagonal,
#{M_{mj}=\beta_m s_j\beta_j}. The counting product of an
indicator with itself is the size #{s_j}. Distinct double
cosets have disjoint indicators by \ref{fdip-012D}, so the
counting product of two different indicators is #{0}. Dividing
each indicator by the positive square root of its size
therefore gives an orthonormal set. The #{(m,j)} entry in that basis is
##{\frac{s_m M_{mj}}{\sqrt{s_m s_j}}
 =M_{mj}\sqrt{\frac{s_m}{s_j}}.}
On the diagonal the two sizes agree, so the square-root factor
equals #{1} and the diagonal remains #{\beta_j^2(1+s_j)}. Off
the diagonal the
product is
##{\beta_m s_j\beta_j\sqrt{\frac{s_m}{s_j}}
 =\beta_m\beta_j\sqrt{s_m s_j}.}
\ref{fdip-0130} places every witness coset at size #{3} or
size #{9}, and #{\sqrt{9}=3}, so every square root lies in the field
generated by #{\sqrt{3}} over the rationals. Each #{\beta_j} is
positive by \ref{fdip-012F} and \ref{fdip-012O}, and each size
is positive, so every entry is positive. Any two distinct
indices are joined by a positive entry.}
}
